@startuml auth
title Authentication: shared Redis sessions and logout
autonumber
actor "User" as User
participant "CLI / TUI" as Client
participant "CLI internal/app" as ClientApp
participant "Document client adapter" as Remote
database "CredentialSessionStore" as Local
box "hermes-document-service" #E0F4E0
participant "API + middleware" as API
participant "internal/app.Auth" as Auth
participant "Other app use cases" as UseCase
participant "internal/credentials" as Verifier
participant "internal/postgres" as Repo
participant "internal/redis\nSessionStore" as Sessions
end box
database "Document PostgreSQL" as DB
database "Redis sessions" as Redis
participant "notification-service" as HNS

== Sign in ==
User -> Client : auth login; provide credentials securely
Client -> ClientApp : Auth.Login
ClientApp -> Remote : Authenticate(credentials)
Remote -> API : TLS request; no session required
API -> Auth : Authenticate(validated credentials)
Auth -> Repo : Read active account and credential verifier/version
Repo -> DB : SELECT account and credentials
DB --> Repo : Candidate record / not found
Repo --> Auth : Candidate record
Auth -> Verifier : Verify(credentials, stored verifier)
Verifier --> Auth : Valid / invalid
alt Unknown/inactive user or invalid credentials
  Auth --> API : Generic authentication failure
else Valid credentials
  Auth -> Auth : Generate random opaque token; derive token hash
  ref over Auth, Redis
    Check account still eligible and create session through SessionStore.
    Serialize with disable/delete and credential changes (ADR-001).
    Exact Redis fields / mass revocation / cross-store protocol remain OPEN.
  end ref
  Auth -> Sessions : Create(token_hash, user_id, expiry) under agreed eligibility protocol
  Sessions -> Redis : Write authoritative active session; expiry enforced
  Redis --> Sessions : Stored / unavailable / unknown outcome
  Sessions --> Auth : Result
  Auth --> API : Raw token + expiry only on confirmed creation / unavailable
end
API --> Remote : Response DTO / error
Remote --> ClientApp : Session / error
alt Confirmed session received
  ClientApp -> Local : Store token, expiry and server identity securely
  alt Local persistence confirmed
    Local --> ClientApp : Saved
    ClientApp --> Client : Signed in
  else Local persistence failed
    Local --> ClientApp : Error
    ClientApp -> Remote : Best-effort Logout(new token) through normal logout path
    Remote --> ClientApp : Revocation confirmed / unknown
    ClientApp --> Client : Sign-in not saved; report cleanup outcome
  end
else No confirmed session
  ClientApp --> Client : Error; no saved login
end
note over ClientApp, Redis
  One opaque token, no refresh token; server stores only its hash.
  Never log secrets or pass credentials as CLI arguments.
  Credential method, TTL and exact session contract remain OPEN.
end note

== Protected document-service request ==
Client -> ClientApp : Execute protected use case
ClientApp -> Remote : Operation(request)
Remote -> Local : Read session for selected server
Local --> Remote : Token / absent
alt No local token
  Remote --> ClientApp : Sign-in required; no network operation
else Token available
  Remote -> API : TLS request with token in headers / metadata
  API -> Auth : ValidateSession(token) from middleware
  Auth -> Sessions : Validate(hash(token))
  Sessions -> Redis : Check active session and expiry under shared contract
  Redis --> Sessions : Identity / invalid / unavailable
  Sessions --> Auth : Validation result
  Auth --> API : Authenticated identity / unauthenticated / unavailable
  alt Valid session
    API -> UseCase : Invoke(request, authenticated identity)
    UseCase -> Repo : Read current domain state / transactional mutation
    Repo -> DB : Current membership and object state
    Repo --> UseCase : State / persistence result
    UseCase --> API : Result / denied / conflict
    API --> Remote : Result / application error
  else Invalid session
    API --> Remote : Unauthenticated; do not execute use case
    Remote --> ClientApp : Unauthenticated
    ClientApp -> Local : Remove rejected token only if still current
  else Redis unavailable
    API --> Remote : Temporarily unavailable; do not execute use case
  end
  Remote --> ClientApp : Mapped result / error
end
note over ClientApp, API
  Permission denied or network failure does not clear the token.
  Closing CLI/TUI does not revoke the session; there is no automatic refresh.
end note

== Connect to notification-service ==
ClientApp -> HNS : WebSocket handshake with same token over TLS
HNS -> Redis : Validate hash and expiry using shared session contract
alt Valid
  Redis --> HNS : Authenticated user_id and session identity
  HNS --> ClientApp : Connection established
else Invalid
  Redis --> HNS : Invalid session
  HNS --> ClientApp : Unauthenticated; connection rejected
else Redis unavailable
  HNS --> ClientApp : Temporarily unavailable; connection rejected
end
note over HNS, Redis
  HNS reads session contract directly; no HNS -> HDS ValidateSession call.
  Authentication does not establish workspace rights.
  Revocation/expiry on already-open sockets remains OPEN (ADR-001).
end note

== Logout current session ==
User -> Client : auth logout / Sign out in TUI
Client -> ClientApp : Auth.Logout
ClientApp -> Local : Read current token
Local --> ClientApp : Token / absent
alt No token
  ClientApp --> Client : Already signed out locally
else Token available
  ClientApp -> Remote : Logout(token)
  Remote -> API : Logout over TLS
  API -> Auth : Logout(token); allow missing/expired session
  Auth -> Sessions : Revoke(hash(token))
  Sessions -> Redis : Remove/invalidate current session if present
  Redis --> Sessions : Revoked / already absent / unavailable
  Sessions --> Auth : Result
  Auth --> API : Signed out only on confirmed revocation/absence / unavailable
  API --> Remote : Result (or network outcome unknown)
  Remote --> ClientApp : Confirmed / failed / unknown
  ClientApp -> Local : Remove matching token, never a newer login
  ClientApp -> ClientApp : Stop authenticated client activity; close local WebSocket
  ClientApp --> Client : Signed out / local-only sign-out / local cleanup failure
end
note over Auth, HNS
  Redis revocation prevents subsequent successful validation.
  Other open sockets and requests already authorized require an explicit policy.
  Failure to confirm revocation means copied tokens may remain usable until expiry.
  Login/logout produce no domain notification events.
end note
@enduml
